[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked *known: one issue, one fix queued*. The reports merged into it
describe two problems with two different fixes. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October; each problem
ended its own way.

## Outcome

| problem | ticket | outcome |
|---|---|---|
| `parser` | [what-it-shows#1](https://github.com/priceotm/what-it-shows/issues/1) | **fixed**: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` |
| `hint` | [what-it-shows#2](https://github.com/priceotm/what-it-shows/issues/2) | **won't fix**: the `booking-v3` form is retired |

## What was merged, by cause

The desk's own count, from the export:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## The two problems

**1 · `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first** →
[what-it-shows#1](https://github.com/priceotm/what-it-shows/issues/1).
Every tenant in the directory writes the day first, so a date like 7/4 comes out as 4 July instead of
7 April. It hits the app, email and the `booking-v2` form. This is what #4471 says, and the queued fix is for it.

**2 · `hint`: the `booking-v3` form tells people to type month first** →
[what-it-shows#2](https://github.com/priceotm/what-it-shows/issues/2).
Its date field shows `MM/DD/YYYY`, but the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. People type
what the hint says, and the booking lands a month off. Only tenants on `booking-v3` get it, and only
through the form. The table above already marks it: *not this ticket, no ticket of its own*. Now it has one.

The note under the table counts reports the desk read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`). They are
filed under `parser` by tenant and belong to what-it-shows#1; if the parser fix does not stop them, they need a closer look.

<Callout>
Both tickets are closed. New date reports after 5 October are not #4471: triage them as new.
</Callout>

## How this was read

- **The desk's view, as exported.** The counts come from `CauseTable` over the exported reports: one id
  is one report, and the cause comes from the tenant's locale and form, not from the intake tag.
- **Not by subject.** [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone, and
  the two problems share subjects ("Booking on the wrong date", "Date is wrong"). That is how they
  ended up in one ticket. It said so itself: "engineering merges them later". This page is that split.

## What the desk can do next

- Consider a decision record that replaces 0001 for incidents: merge on cause (tenant, channel, form),
  not on subject. The [triage explorer](/kb/triage/dedupe) already shows pairs with the same subject and
  different causes.

